<section id="A1.SS11" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.11 </span>Experiments on more models</h3>

<div id="A1.SS11.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection, we test the effectiveness of our method on several advanced LVLMs. We select Qwen2.5-VL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, InternVL3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, Mantis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., </a>)</cite>, and Idefics3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laurençon et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and conduct experiments on MME and CHAIR using greedy search. The experimental results are shown in Table <a href="#A1.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ A.11 Experiments on more models ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Dynamic Multimodal Activation Steering for Hallucination Mitigation in Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>. Our method remains effective for these advanced LVLMs with different architectures, demonstrating hallucination mitigation effects on both discriminative tasks and open-ended generation tasks, which confirms the scalability of our approach.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T13" class="ltx_table">
<div id="A1.T13.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:309.7pt;height:123.2pt;vertical-align:-59.1pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1.0,1.0) ;">
