<section id="A1.SS12" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.12 </span>Ablation studies on hallucination objects selection strategies</h3>

<div id="A1.SS12.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For visual perception attention heads, we compare two hallucination objects selection strategies: (1) random selection across categories (e.g., people → panda), and (2) hard negatives within the same category (e.g., runner → swimmer).
As shown in the Table <a href="#A1.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ A.12 Ablation studies on hallucination objects selection strategies ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Dynamic Multimodal Activation Steering for Hallucination Mitigation in Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, experiments on MME and CHAIR benchmarks demonstrate that both strategies achieve comparable performance with no significant difference. This indicates that our visual steering vector approach is robust to the choice of negative selection strategy.</p>
</div>
